 ## Background
-The discrete nature of 3D Gaussian Splatting often results in geometry that is difficult to extract as a clean mesh, leading to "popping" artifacts and floating structures [S1]. Early attempts to mitigate this included tile-based sorting (StopThePop) [S1]. Subsequent work introduced explicit geometric constraints: GaussianPro used normal consistency and plane constraints [S1]; NeuSG and GSDF incorporated neural SDFs to enforce zero-level set alignment [S1]; and sparse-view methods leveraged pre-trained 2D depth estimators to supervise geometry [S1]. More recently, scale alignment techniques have addressed the metric scale ambiguity inherent in image-only reconstruction pipelines [S2, unofficial].
+The original 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) method [Kerbl et al., 2023] focuses on high-quality novel view synthesis but lacks explicit surface representation, resulting in "floating" artifacts and poor geometry [S1]. To mitigate this, subsequent works introduced geometric priors. NeuSG [S1] was among the first to transfer the NeuS implicit surface framework to 3DGS. GSDF [S1][S2, unofficial] and 3DGSR [S1] further explored SDF consistency. Sparse-view methods [S1] leveraged monocular depth estimators to solve ambiguity. More recent works like GaussianPro [S1] and DN-Splatter [S1] incorporate normal consistency and multi-view constraints. The challenge of scale ambiguity in monocular depth priors led to scale alignment techniques [S2, unofficial]. These methods are widely used in applications requiring mesh extraction, such as SLAM [9268-slam-and-mapping.md] and application-specific reconstructions [9269-application-specific-reconstructions.md].
 
